Make LÖVE build for visionOS, render without a presentable layer, and boot
from a host that owns @main.

Part 1 -- building at all. visionOS defines TARGET_OS_IPHONE, so config.h
takes the LOVE_IOS branch and the iOS backend compiles, which is what we want:
Metal, the iOS filesystem layout, the SDL3 UIKit path. Three things stop it:

  1. common/ios.mm uses UIScreen in showGameList; visionOS has none, because
     there is no single "main screen" to take bounds from. That function is
     LÖVE's picker for several .love files and is unreachable for a bundle
     with one fused game.love, so it only has to compile.
  2. graphics/Graphics.cpp calls opengl::createInstance() unconditionally while
     the Vulkan and Metal backends beside it are already guarded. Every GLES
     symbol in XROS.sdk is API_UNAVAILABLE(visionos).
  3. Hence a matching LOVE_GRAPHICS_OPENGL macro in config.h, defined
     everywhere except visionOS. Worth upstreaming on its own: it makes "a
     platform with no OpenGL backend" expressible, which is a gap today.

Part 2 -- rendering with nowhere to present. A fully immersive visionOS app
has no CAMetalLayer; frames go to CompositorServices drawables and the flat 2D
image becomes a texture shown in a window or in-world. metal::Graphics::setMode
now accepts a null context and stands an offscreen texture in for the drawable.
Render passes, MSAA resolve and screenshot readback keep working against it;
only presentDrawable is skipped. The texture is MTLStorageModeShared so the
host can read it back without staging through a blit -- unified memory makes
that free on Apple GPUs.

This is a RUNTIME choice, not an #ifdef, and that matters: the flat visionOS
build still presents through SDL's layer exactly as on iOS and must keep
working. Window.cpp consults love_visionos_getHeadless().

Part 3 -- booting from a host that owns @main. love_visionos_boot() runs LÖVE
without main(), for a SwiftUI shell that has its own entry point; LOVE_NO_MAIN
suppresses LÖVE's main() and, with it, SDL_main.h's takeover
(SDL_MAIN_HANDLED), which otherwise leaves an undefined _SDL_main.

love_visionos_prepareOnMainThread() exists because SDL's video init reaches
into UIKit and BoardServices asserts a main-run-loop barrier there. It is
necessary but NOT sufficient: SDL's window creation calls UIKit too and traps
the same way, so LÖVE cannot yet run on a thread of its own while SDL owns a
window.

